## Caching

To begin, only do caching when you really need to. It is a source of bugs and create situations that lead to serious problems for our customers. For example, I've set up caching with an app with different user roles and sent the wrong data to the wrong user as that was the data in the cache. Therefore, you want to be deliberate with caching.

Then I would suggest to think of caching in two areas. The server and the UI. TanStack Query will cache on the frontend for you and this will do a great job in terms of an individual users experience. On the server you could use redis which will cache across multiple users and across multiple sessions.

First start with ensuring TanStack Query is set up well for the use case. Then I would look to add the server side caching only for the most critical endpoints that are slowest to compute.

## Mise-en-place

[Mise-en-place](https://mise.jdx.dev/getting-started.html) is a tool to help manage development environments. I started using this
as I have started to use multiple programming languages to deliver applications.

In this case, we are using `bun` to develop the frontend and `python` to develop the backend.

```zsh
curl https://mise.run | sh
```

It's then best to activate mise for your shell. For example, if you are using `zsh`:

```zsh
eval "$(mise activate zsh --shims)" >> ~/.zshrc
source ~/.zshrc
```

Then check
```zsh
export PATH="$HOME/.local/share/mise/shims:$PATH"
```

was successfully added to your `.zshrc` file and check the PATH is correct by running:

```zsh
echo $PATH
```

Then use `mise doctor` just validate everything is working. In what is a frustrating confusion,
there are two ways to activate mise, I've used the SHIMS method above

The above is important as for programs to run, the OS needs to know where they are so they can run the executables.
If you install these tools directly, the install process will alter the PATH for you. Mise with SHIMS will
add small wrapper scripts that intercept calls to these programs and redirect them to the correct version.

SHIMS is said to work better with IDEs and the PATH method is said to be better with terminal useage.
I've not seen a good illustrating example however. [More info here](https://mise.jdx.dev/ide-integration.html).

If you get the error `mise ERROR no tasks defined in ~. Are you in a project directory?`, then
this could be a number of things and none of which are releated to the actual message itself.
I would go consult the docs instead to ensure you have everything set up correctly.

Once this is in place, it will be done forever for your shell so it's only a one time set up.

Then you can run commands like:

```zsh
mise use uv@latest bun@latest --global
```

This will install the latest versions of `uv` and `bun` and you can now run these commands directly from your terminal.


1. https://lightningcss.dev/
